            {
              "type": "reasoning.summary",
              "summary": "**Evaluating confidence in reports**\n\nI'm considering how to interpret peer reports as direct confidence with probabilities. If I multiply likelihood ratios, the confidence levels can suggest correctness. However, there's a caveat that these confidence values aren't guarantees. It’s essential to treat stated confidence as a soft report, but there's no model for calibration here. The generative model correlates surveys with the analysts’ local data, yet I shouldn’t just assume their confidence reflects true probabilities. It’s possible they generate those values intentionally.**Identifying intended aggregation**\n\nI’m trying to figure out how to identify the intended aggregation from the data. Each peer has three survey observations, and their confidence could represent a posterior probability after seeing those observations, except for one anomaly at .685715. Now, I wonder why this particular value is anomalous. Could it be that this confidence actually comes from a different generated forecast that isn’t constrained? This seems like an important detail to clarify for accurate analysis.",
